WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.530
I'm on my way back from the Arctic,

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.439
but there is time for one more guest video!

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:05.690
And it's from Inés at Draw Curiosity,

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.840
who's talking about those weird floaty things

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:08.840
that you get in your eye sometimes.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.540
So Tom's taken his eyes off his channel and
he's invited me to talk to you

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.990
about 4 visual phenomena and disturbances
that we all experience,

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.310
and you don't have to go particularly far
for any of them.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:23.599
For the first one, all you need to do is to
look at a blank wall, or up at the sky,

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:27.400
and you'll probably notice some dark grey
spots floating around.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.009
These are floaters and what you're seeing
are the shadows cast

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:33.489
by bits of debris which have come loose from
the back of the eye.

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:34.940
They come in all shapes and sizes

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.250
because the proteins and the cells can clump
together in different manners

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:43.020
and you can even estimate whereabouts in the
eye they are based on their appearance.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.579
The sharper the shadows appear to be,

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:46.239
the closer they are to the retina,

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:50.370
and those that are blurrier and less defined
are closer to the centre of the eye.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.460
Although the can be a bit annoying,

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.489
as long as they haven't appeared suddenly,
they're completely harmless.

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:59.100
Whilst you're still up looking at the sky
you'll probably also notice some white spots

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.630
floating around.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.809
This is known as the blue entoptic phenomenon,

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:05.430
and what you're seeing are your white blood
cells being pumped through

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.360
a network of blood vessels that's in front
of the retina.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.930
Our brain actually does a great job of correcting the
shadows cast by these blood vessels onto the retina,

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.450
but because they're composed of a majority
of red blood cells,

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.340
it means that the white blood cells which
are a different colour,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.079
shape and size don't get filtered out as well.

00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:25.880
But much like the floaters, they don't really
affect everyday vision.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.579
Now so far, the two things I've told you about
are real objects found inside our eyes,

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:34.320
but the next two are artefacts that aren't
there at all.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.540
Visual snow is a phenomenon that everyone
reports experiencing to some degree,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.860
and it's the appearance of a static or grainy
like texture over all or parts of the visual field.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.250
Personally, I find it to be the most noticeable
over dark colours or in the dark,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.520
and it reminds me of footage shot on a camera
with a high ISO.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.820
High ISO refers to a high sensitivity setting
on the sensor,

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.940
which means that is a lot more sensitive to
light,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.880
but also a lot more prone to noise,

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.060
which you can see in the grainy footage around
me.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.600
Now whilst we can explain grainy texture in
cameras,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.060
we don't actually know what causes visual
snow

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.680
but we believe it to be neural,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.010
which means it has a lot more to do with the
neurons and the way nerves process these signals,

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.790
as opposed to the eye structure itself.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.900
And last, but not least, are phosphenes.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.510
I'm sure you've heard the expression "to see
stars",

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.500
and if you've ever received a blow to the
head,

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.880
or you've coughed particularly hard,

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.120
you know what that expression means, those
are phosphenes.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.830
Likewise, if you've ever rubbed your eyes,
or tapped the sides of them,

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:34.300
you've probably seen some pretty geometric
shapes appear before them,

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.470
and these are also phosphenes.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.090
Generally, the images that we see

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.480
are the result of light stimulating our photoreceptors
at the back of the eye,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.530
but they can also be triggered in other manners,
for instance, mechanically.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:49.340
If I tap the side of my eye, I'm actually
changing the pressure inside it,

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.760
and this causes some of the photoreceptors
to fire

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.870
and cause those sparks and pretty geometric
shapes that we see.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.360
We also know that phosphenes can arise in
the brain.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.920
People who take psychedelic drugs also report
seeing phosphenes,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.920
and some scientists found that if you pass
a gentle electric current

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.930
through the brain's visual cortex,

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.780
you will also report seeing some pretty geometric
shapes.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.171
These were the 4 disturbances that I wanted
to talk to you about today,

00:03:13.171 --> 00:03:14.870
and I think it just goes to show

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.670
how complex and fascinating our visual system
really is,

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.180
and adds a whole new layer of meaning to the
expression,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.490
"there's plenty more, than meets the eye"

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.070
Inés, thank you very much!

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.410
Go subscribe to Draw Curiosity,

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.210
there are links on screen or in the description
now.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.130
Also in the description is a link to Inés'
blog post

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.940
with the full research notes and bibliography
for the video you've just seen.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.230
That is it for the guest videos!

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.960
Unless I've been eaten by a polar bear,

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.430
in which case this will be a very ironic end
tag,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.900
I am back next week, and I'll see you then.

